 a new point in the tradeoffs among setup, complexity assumptions, proof size, and computational cost.\nL37: We apply the Fiat-Shamir heuristic to this argument to produce a zero-knowledge succinct non-interactive argument of knowledge (zkSNARK) in the random oracle model, based on the discrete log assumption, which we call Hyrax. We implement Hyrax and evaluate it against five state-of-the-art baseline systems.\nL38: Our evaluation shows that, even for modest problem sizes, Hyrax gives smaller proofs than all but the most computationally costly baseline, and that its prover and verifier are each faster than three of the five baselines.\nL39: Note: This version adds a link to the released source code.\nL40: ##### Metadata\nL41: \nL42: Available format(s)\nL43:     【17†PDF】\nL44: Category\nL45:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL46: Publication info\nL47:     Published elsewhere. Major revision. IEEE Security & Privacy 2018\nL48: Keywords\nL49:     【19†zero knowledge】【20†succinct arguments】【21†computationally-sound proofs】\nL50: Contact author(s)\nL51:     rsw @ cs stanford edu\nL52: History\nL53:     2018-04-19: last of 5 revisions\nL54:     2017-11-27: received\nL55:     【22†See all versions】\nL56: Short URL\nL57:     【23†https://ia.cr/2017/1132†ia.cr】\nL58: License\nL59: \nL60: 【24†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL61: BibTeX 【25†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL62: \nL63:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2017/1132,\nL64:           author = {Riad S.  Wahby and Ioanna Tzialla and abhi shelat and Justin Thaler and Michael Walfish},\nL65:           title = {Doubly-efficient {zkSNARKs} without trusted setup},\nL66:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2017/1132},\nL67:           year = {2017},\nL68:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/1132}\nL69:     }\nL70: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInteractive Proofs for Batch Polynomial Evaluation (https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/448)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/448\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 81\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2026/448\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Interactive Proofs for Batch Polynomial Evaluation】\nL32: \nL33: Gal Arnon, Bocconi University\nL34: \nL35: Alessandro Chiesa, École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne\nL36: \nL37: Giacomo Fenzi, École Polytechnique Fédérale de Lausanne\nL38: \nL39: Eylon Yogev, Bar-Ilan University\nL40: ##### Abstract\nL41: Polynomials are a fundamental mathematical object underlying virtually all of theoretical computer science. In proof systems, a common task for the verifier is to evaluate a polynomial of degree $d$ at $m$ distinct points. The best known algorithm for this problem performs $O((m + d) \\cdot \\log^2(m + d))$ field operations.\nL42: We present a concretely efficient $\\mathsf{MA}$ protocol for this problem in which the verifier runs in \\emph{linear time}: the prover sends a single message consisting of $d - 1$ field elements, and the verifier performs only $O(m + d)$ field operations. We further extend our protocol to handle the more general setting of evaluating multiple polynomials at multiple points, and for this problem, we construct an $\\mathsf{AMA}$ protocol. Our protocols improve the verifier time in several interactive proofs.\nL43: Most notably are the sumcheck protocol over a large summation domain and protocols that rely on polynomial quotienting. In particular, by a straightforward application of our results, we reduce the verifier's runtime in the STIR protocol (CRYPTO 2024) to match that of WHIR (EUROCRYPT 2025), despite WHIR being highly optimized for verification time.\nL44: As an additional application, we show that any univariate polynomial commitment schemes (PCS) can be transformed, in a black-box manner, into a new scheme that efficiently supports batch openings at multiple points. In particular, opening $m$ points incurs only a constant overhead compared to opening a single point.\nL45: ##### Metadata\nL46: \nL47: Available format(s)\nL48:     【17†PDF】\nL49: Category\nL50:     【18†Cryptographic protocols】\nL51: Publication info\nL52:     Preprint.\nL53: Keywords\nL54:     【19†interactive proofs】【20†polynomial evaluation】\nL55: Contact author(s)\nL56:     galarnon42 @ gmail com\nL57: alessandro chiesa @ epfl ch\nL58: giacomo fenzi @ epfl ch\nL59: eylon yogev @ biu ac il\nL60: History\nL61:     2026-03-05: revised\nL62:     2026-03-04: received\nL63:     【21†See all versions】\nL64: Short URL\nL65:     【22†https://ia.cr/2026/448†ia.cr】\nL66: License\nL67: \nL68: 【23†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL69: BibTeX 【24†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL70: \nL71:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2026/448,\nL72:           author = {Gal Arnon and Alessandro Chiesa and Giacomo Fenzi and Eylon Yogev},\nL73:           title = {Interactive Proofs for Batch Polynomial Evaluation},\nL74:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2026/448},\nL75:           year = {2026},\nL76:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/448}\nL77:     }\nL78: \nL79: \nL80: Note: In order to